## Overview

No code reaches production without going through a pull request. PRs serve as the primary mechanism for code review, quality assurance, and knowledge sharing across the team. Every PR — regardless of size — must be reviewed and approved before merging.

---

## Branch Strategy

- The `main` (or `production`) branch is **protected**. Direct pushes are disabled.
- All work must be done on a feature, bugfix, or hotfix branch branched from `main`.
- Use the following naming conventions:

  | Type    | Pattern                        | Example                        |
  |---------|--------------------------------|--------------------------------|
  | Feature | `feature/<short-description>`  | `feature/user-auth-flow`       |
  | Bugfix  | `fix/<short-description>`      | `fix/login-redirect-loop`      |
  | Hotfix  | `hotfix/<short-description>`   | `hotfix/payment-null-pointer`  |
  | Chore   | `chore/<short-description>`    | `chore/update-dependencies`    |

- Keep branches short-lived. Long-running branches increase merge conflicts and drift from `main`.

---

## Opening a Pull Request

1. **Ensure your branch is up to date** with `main` before opening the PR. Rebase or merge `main` into your branch to resolve conflicts locally first.
2. **Open the PR against `main`** using the standard PR template (see below).
3. **Fill out the template fully.** Incomplete descriptions or unchecked checklist items will result in the PR being returned to the author.
4. **Link related issues** using GitHub keywords (e.g. `Closes #123`) so issues close automatically on merge.
5. **Assign reviewers** — at least one required reviewer must be assigned before the PR is considered open for review.
6. **Add appropriate labels** (e.g. `bug`, `enhancement`, `security`, `dependencies`).

### PR Template

```markdown
## Description
Briefly describe changes

## 🔒 Security Review
- [ ] I have reviewed the code for common vulnerabilities (OWASP Top 10: SQLi, XSS, etc.).
- [ ] I have verified that this code does not introduce secrets (API keys, passwords, tokens) into the repo.
- [ ] I have considered the impact of this change on authorization/authentication.
- [ ] I have validated all user input to prevent injection attacks.
- [ ] If changing data handling, I have considered GDPR/PII implications.

## 🧪 Testing & Validation
- [ ] I have added tests for this change.
- [ ] I have performed a self-review of the code.
- [ ] I have manually verified the functionality.

## 📦 Dependencies
- [ ] I have checked that no new, unapproved dependencies have been added.
- [ ] I have updated `requirements.txt` if necessary.
```

---

## PR Checklist

Before marking a PR as **Ready for Review**, the author must confirm:

- [ ] The PR title clearly describes the change (e.g. `Fix null pointer in payment handler`, not `Fix bug`).
- [ ] All checklist items in the PR template are addressed.
- [ ] CI/CD pipelines are passing (linting, tests, build).
- [ ] No debug code, commented-out code, or `TODO`s are left in without a linked issue.
- [ ] All new code is covered by appropriate unit and/or integration tests.
- [ ] Documentation has been updated if the change affects public APIs, configuration, or behaviour.

